.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_destroy \- destroy a memory pool and set pointer to NULL
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

ccol_mempool_destroy(mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Frees every resource of the memory pool. For a pool created from a
preallocated buffer the macro frees only the pool struct, not the
buffer itself.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp   Memory pool to destroy. The macro sets it to NULL afterward
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Calling the macro with a NULL pointer is safe
For a preallocated pool, the buffer's lifetime is the caller's business

The macro evaluates mp exactly once. It must be a modifiable lvalue,
such as a variable or an element of an array, so with an expression
that has a side effect, such as handles[i++], it destroys and clears
one element.
.fi
.PP
The library can call the free function of a custom allocator after this macro
returns, and even after main() returns. A pool that threads share keeps a small
cache of entries for each thread. A destroy of the pool marks those caches dead
but doesn't free them, because a cache belongs to the thread that holds it and
another thread must not free it under that thread. Each cache is freed later,
at one of three points: when the owning thread next has to get a cache for a
pool that it doesn't already hold one for, when that thread exits, or at
process exit or when dlclose(3) unloads the library, if that thread is the one
that runs the library's unload handler. Each free calls the free function that
the pool was created with. None of these points is guaranteed to come: a thread
that keeps using the same few pools answers every request from the caches it
already holds and never gets another one, so it can hold a dead cache for ever.
A program that needs its allocator to see every free should join such threads.
.PP
Once dlclose(3) unloads the library the code that frees a cache is gone, so the
cache of every other thread that is still running stays allocated for the rest
of the process, while that thread itself exits cleanly. If their caches must go
back to the allocator, join the threads that used a thread-safe pool before you
unload the library.
.PP
So an allocator that is torn down at a known point must outlive that point. The
library can call an allocator that draws from an arena after the end of main()
has freed that arena, and the same goes for an allocator that draws from
storage which the application's exit handler frees. An allocator built on
malloc and free isn't affected, and neither is one built on storage that lives
for the whole process. A pool created with single_threaded keeps no such cache
and has no such tail.
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
A pointer from the dynamic fallback that was never freed makes the
destroy fail an assertion, so free every allocated entry before you
destroy the pool.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3),
.BR ccol_r_mempool_destroy (3)
